                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="867-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_c.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Cardioid" title="Cardioid" ></span>CAD Audio equitek e60</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Cardioid Condenser Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The e60 is a medium-diaphragm solid-state condenser microphone in an unusually rugged package.</p><p>The capsule measures 16mm in diameter and utilizes a gold-sputtered diaphragm. It is a &#8220;true condenser&#8221; (externally biased) design.</p><p>The amplifier circuit is a next-generation version of CAD&#8217;s &#8220;equitek&#8221; circuit, which uses op-amps rather than transistors or transformers in the output circuit. Earlier mics from the equitek line required internal batteries to provide high current to the op-amps during transients, but in the e60 the circuit has been redesigned to run fine on 24V phantom power. </p><p>Intended primarily as an instrument microphone, the e60 is suggested by CAD for use on drum OH, acoustic guitar, hi-hat, and brass. </p><p>The mic has a 4-way high-pass filter, providing selectable low-frequency rolloff at 6dB/octave at 40Hz, 85Hz, or 122Hz.</p><p>A second switch provides a -10dB non-capacitive pad.</p><p>The mic was introduced at Summer NAMM 2004.<p>Permalink: <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/CAD/e60">CAD Audio e60</a></p><p>The mic was released in 2004.</p>
